   246	sources bypass the network allowlist.
   247	
   248	### 6.2 Git safety and references
   249	
   250	Git execution MUST restrict protocols to `file`, `git`, `http`, `https`, and
   251	`ssh`; refuse empty or dash-prefixed operands; and place untrusted operands
   252	after `--` where supported. Submodules are unsupported. Archive extraction
   253	MUST reject symbolic links, hard links, path escapes, duplicate platform paths,
   254	and entries exceeding implementation limits documented by the manager.
   255	
   256	Tags resolve `refs/tags/<value>^{commit}`. Revisions resolve
   257	`<value>^{commit}`. Branches prefer `refs/remotes/origin/<value>` and then a
   258	local head. A resolved commit is the full lowercase hexadecimal object id
   259	returned by git. Snapshots are immutable regular-file trees produced from that
   260	commit.
   261	
   262	## 7. Closure resolution
   263	
   264	Direct declarations enter as `full` requirements from synthetic consumer
   265	`<project>`. Processing a provider adds its skill requirements.
   266	
   267	Within one closure, one skill name MUST resolve to exactly one commit and one
   268	canonical source identity. Different identities or commits fail with every
   269	relevant requirement chain. Different refs resolving to one commit unify.
   270	Cycles fail and name the cycle.
   271	
   272	Activation is edge-based. Context is active when any incoming edge is `full`
   273	or `context`. All commands are active when any edge is `full`; otherwise the
   274	active set is the union of runtime edges, narrowed where requested.
   275	
   276	Provider order is deterministic Kahn topological order: among currently ready
   277	providers select the lexicographically smallest skill name by Unicode scalar
   278	value. The synthetic project is not emitted. Diagnostic requirement chains use
   279	the lexicographically smallest complete chain when multiple chains are equal
   280	in length.
   281	
   282	## 8. Content hashes
   283	
   284	The Curator content hash is calculated over regular files excluding the marker
   285	itself. For each selected file in sorted protocol-path order, append:
   286	
   287	```text
   288	UTF8(path) || 0x00 || file_bytes
   289	```
   290	
   291	Join adjacent records with one additional `0x00`, hash the resulting byte
   292	string with SHA-256, encode lowercase hexadecimal, and prefix `sha256:`. The
   293	empty tree hashes the empty byte string. File mode, owner, timestamp, and
   294	filesystem-native separator are not hashed. Readers MUST reject duplicate
   295	protocol paths rather than hash one arbitrarily.
   296	
   297	## 9. Install markers
   298	
   299	Every installed closure node has `.csk-install.json` conforming to
   300	`install-marker-v1.schema.json`. `locale` is always present and is a string or
   301	`null`. Required set-like arrays are always arrays, including when empty.
   302	
   303	The following arrays are set-like and writers sort them by Unicode scalar
   304	value: `agents`, `commands`, `dependencies`, `files`, `requirements`,
   305	`runtime_roots`, `requirers`, `activation.commands`, and every MCP agent list.
   306	Object member order and whitespace are not significant.
   307	
   308	An installation is current only when the marker schema is supported; ref kind,
   309	ref, commit, locale, agents, activation, substitution, MCP findings, and
   310	attestation match the effective plan; and the installed content hash matches
   311	`content_sha256`. Unsupported or unreadable markers are not current. A moved
   312	tag is a warning, or an error under strict-tag policy.
   313	
   314	The marker is not a signature and MUST NOT be used as an authorization token.
   315	Registry attestations are reverified from signed records when fresh trust is
   316	required.
   317	
   318	## 10. Adapter ledger
   319	
   320	Every managed adapter root contains `.csk-managed.json` conforming to
   321	`adapter-ledger-v1.schema.json`. `entries` is a sorted unique list of skill
   322	names owned by the manager in that adapter root. A manager MUST remove only
   323	entries in its preceding ledger and MUST fail rather than overwrite an
   324	unmanaged conflicting entry.

